  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss. The steep x direction overshoots when learning rate × 2.5 > 1 and diverges when it is above 2 (learning rate above 0.8).",
    "Batch noise per component: random, centred on the true slope, with standard deviation 1.5 × sqrt((64 − B)/(63 B)). That is 1.5 at B = 1 and exactly 0 at B = 64.",
    "The orange arrow is the step the ball takes next: −learning rate × noisy slope. The grey arrow is −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn 3 times their true length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6, which is the loss at the bowl's corner (diverged). The trail holds the start plus up to 40 step points. The ghost holds one earlier run.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed, so Reset replays the same noise. With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. A diverged ball is drawn at the bowl's edge, and Loss keeps its true value.",
    "The ball takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed (one update every 6 host ticks)."
  ]
}